## Architecture rules

- **Request path:** browser → (Vite in dev) → **edge** → the service that owns the URL prefix (`services.json`, longest prefix wins). Services never call the browser-facing edge; they call each other with `ctx.call("<name>", request)`, which stays in the process when both run in `app-all` and goes over HTTP otherwise. Never `reqwest` + `service_url` for another service.
- **Bridges** (`docs/bridges.md`): a service is a library whose `build(ctx)` returns its router and starts its background work (`tokio::spawn`); `main.rs` stays one call. Services never import each other (only `app/` links them), share nothing in memory (the database or `ctx.call`), get only headers from a request (no `ConnectInfo` or other extensions; the edge passes the client address as `x-client-ip`), never open their own database, and run CPU work on `spawn_blocking`.
- **Layers of a service:** `routes.rs` (extract the caller, parse, call logic, map to JSON) → logic/validation module (`model.rs`) → `store.rs` (SQL only) → SQLite. No SQL in handlers, no HTTP types in the store.
- **Ownership:** only the owner service writes a table (named in a `-- [owner]` comment in its migration). Others may read it.
- **Errors:** handlers return `ApiResult<Json<T>>`; errors are `ApiError` (`bad_request`, `unauthorized`, `forbidden`, `not_found`, `conflict`, `too_many_requests`, `unavailable`, `internal`) and reach the client as `{"error": {"code", "message"}}`. Database errors convert automatically; internal errors are logged and reach the client only as `Internal server error`.
- **Users:** per-user data is scoped by `app_core::Caller` (`caller.user_id`) in every query.{% if features.auth %} Use `AuthUser` when the handler needs the user's details and `AdminUser` for admin-only routes.{% else %} There is no sign-in yet: every request is the local user `local`; adding accounts only changes `crates/core/src/caller.rs`.{% endif %}
- **Responses:** camelCase JSON (`#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]`), times as Unix ms (`app_core::now_ms()`), ids from `app_core::new_id()` (UUID v7), lists as `{"items": [...]}`.
- **Web:** page → feature store (Pinia) → feature `api.ts` → `@/shared/api/http.ts` (the only `fetch`). A feature imports only `@/shared/**` and its own files.

## Security

The defaults protect a public web app. Never weaken them to make something work: fix the cause, or ask the owner{% if options.spec_md %} and record the exception in `SPEC.md`{% endif %}.

- **Headers** (`services/edge/src/headers.rs`, every response): `nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, COOP, `Permissions-Policy`, HSTS when `PUBLIC_URL` is https, a strict CSP for the built app (no inline scripts or styles; `data:` only for images and fonts) and `default-src 'none'` for everything else, `Cache-Control: no-store` with credentials. A new external host for scripts, styles, fonts or `fetch` needs a CSP change with its reason.
{%- if features.auth %}
- **Sessions** (`crates/core/src/auth/`): HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie (`__Host-` and `Secure` behind https), a new token per sign-in, deleted at sign-out; a password change signs out the other sessions. Cookie-signed `POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE` need an allowed `Origin` (`auth/origin.rs`: `PUBLIC_URL`, Vite in development, `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`), else 403; API-key requests are exempt.
- **Sign-in throttling** (`services/identity/src/throttle.rs`): per email and per client, 5 free failures in 15 minutes, then waits from 1 s doubling to 15 minutes; per client 30 sign-ins per 10 minutes and 10 registrations per hour; 429 with `Retry-After`, the same for unknown emails, never a lockout.
{%- endif %}
- **Host check** (`services/edge/src/host.rs`, every request): the edge answers only for `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `[::1]` and the hosts of the allowed origins (`PUBLIC_URL`, Vite, `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`), else 421, so DNS rebinding cannot reach the loopback API. A published app's hostname goes into `PUBLIC_URL`.
- **Client address:** only the edge sets `x-client-ip` (`app_core::client_ip`, `TRUST_PROXY`); use it for limits and logs, never for access decisions.
- **Data:** every query of per-user data is scoped by the owner (`WHERE id = ? AND owner_id = ?`){% if features.auth %} and has a two-user test: user B gets 404 on A's rows and never sees them in lists{% endif %}. Bodies over `MAX_BODY_BYTES` (1 MiB) get 413.
- **Outbound HTTP:** every URL from a user, a model, a webhook setting or a third party goes through `ctx.outbound` (`app_core::outbound`: public addresses only, ports 80/443, redirects re-checked, 10 s, 5 MB). Other services: `ctx.call`. `ctx.http` (plain reqwest) only for fixed, trusted API hosts written in the code.
- **Before touching** authentication, access control, input handling, outbound requests, uploads or LLM features, read `secbase checklist <area>` (`secbase checklist` lists the areas).
- `npm run check` ends with `npm run security` (`secbase check`, profile in `secbase.json`); an exception goes into its `allow` list with a reason.

## Quality walls

`walls` is the model-free code-quality gate: fixed rules, same answer every time. `npm run check` includes `walls check` right before the security step; `npm run walls` runs it alone (`npm run walls -- --json` for the full report). Project settings are in `walls.json`, project walls in `.walls/rules.json`.

- Never weaken the lint, tsc or clippy configuration (rules, `strict`, `--max-warnings`, `-D warnings`) and never add a suppression without a reason. A finding is silenced only with a `walls-ignore <rule>: <reason>` comment on its line or the line above; fix the cause first.
- When a reviewer or the owner points out a mistake for the second time, do not write a note: add a project wall to `.walls/rules.json` (`id` `project/<name>`, `message`, `pattern`, `files` and a `reason` that names the incident), then fix the current hits.
- `walls rules` lists the built-in rules; `walls rules --explain <id>` says why a rule exists. Do not edit `.walls/baseline.json` by hand: `walls baseline` rewrites it. Loosening the gate (a new `ignore` entry `{ "path", "reason" }` or `allow` entry in `walls.json`, a weaker project wall, a bigger baseline) is a `gate/loosened` error until the owner commits it: ask him instead.
- Install walls once: `npm link` in `~/Documents/walls`. Without it the step fails, in CI too; only `WALLS_OPTIONAL=1` turns that into a skip with a notice. Never set it to get a green check.

